    "lawCode": "Amd §66-j, Pub Serv L; amd §1020-g, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A2442C\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public service law and public\nauthorities law, in relation to the net energy metering for micro-com-\nbined heat and power generating systems\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF THE BILL:  This legislation would expand New\nYork's net metering law by adding residential Micro-CHP systems to the\nlist of currently eligible technologies that can be net metered.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:  Subdivision 1 of §66-j of the Public Service Law\nis amended to provide that residential Micro-CHP systems may be eligible\nfor net metering under certain circumstances.\n\nA new subdivision 1(f) is added to §66-j of the public service law that\ndefines Micro-CHP systems.\n\nThe bill also amends §66-j of the Public Service Law and subdivision (h)\nof §1020-g of the Public Authorities Law to require electric corpo-\nrations to permit net energy metering for Micro-CHP generating equip-\nment, and to provide for the interconnection to the electric system of\nMicro-CHP generating equipment in the same manner as other technologies.\nUnlike other systems which are subject to net metering, however, in the\nevent a Micro-CHP customer-generator produces more electricity than they\ncan use during a billing period, the customer will be credited for any\nexcess only at the corporation's avoided costs.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  New York State and its residents face significant energy\nand climate change challenges. Energy efficiency continues to be the\nmost cost-effective, and the most immediate way to reduce the burden of\nrising energy and environmental costs as well as our energy-related\ncarbon footprint. The Micro-CHP technology is at the forefront of energy\nefficiency. It is essentially a small scale cogeneration device provid-\ning both heat and electricity with the same amount of fuel needed to\noperate a standard furnace. Proliferation of these devices brings about\nbenefits not only to the homeowner, but also to utility corporations and\nsociety as a whole. Consider the benefits:\n\nEnergy efficiency - typical central power generation delivers only\n30-35% of fuel energy; 55-65% is lost as waste heat and the remainder is\nlost as a normal function of transmission and distribution losses.  In\ncontrast, a Micro-CHP unit utilizes over 90% of the fuel for fuel ener-\ngy. This will result in a 36% reduction in fuel consumption.  Looked at\nfrom a macro-perspective, the US Department of Energy estimates that if\n20% of US energy needs were met by CHP it would be the equivalent of\ntaking 154 million cars off of US roads.\n\nReduced greenhouse gas emissions - lowers the carbon footprint as\nfollows:\n\n* 86% reduction in NOx;\n\n* 100% reduction in S02; and\n\n* 46% reduction in CO2.\n\nFurthermore, Micro-CHP has been identified by the US Environmental\nProtection Agency as one of just two technologies to achieve a \"Climate\nChoice\" rating under the Energy Star Program. Climate Choice is a new\npartnership program that recognizes emerging technologies that have the\npotential to substantially reduce greenhouse gas emissions once they are\nmore widely adopted.\n\nGrid enhancement - Distributed generation enhances grid reliability and\nsupports the grid in peak demand times through demand response.\n\nEconomic Stimulus - Micro-CHP systems are built right here in New York\nState. The US Department of Energy estimates that if 20% of US energy\nneeds were met by CHP by 2030 it would generate $234B in new investment\nand would create one million new highly skilled jobs. With cutting-edge\ntechnology already underway here, New York could be at the forefront of\nthe benefits arising from this technology.\n\nIn sum, using a more efficient process to supply both heat and power to\nhomes promotes energy efficiency and creates a path toward energy inde-\npendence as we increase our nation's energy security. By essentially\ndoubling the benefits received from each unit of fuel used, residential\nenergy consumers will now be able to achieve significantly improved\nenergy efficiency, reduce their energy costs and reduce pollutant emis-\nsions all at the same time. Micro-CHP systems can achieve most of the\nenvironmental benefits of renewable energy systems at a fraction of the\ncost.\n\nThe bill is necessary to encourage investments in Micro-CHP systems.\nThese systems will provide significant benefits to residential consumers\nsuch as reduced energy costs and will help the state achieve its goal of\nreduced emissions as well. Additionally, the bill will help achieve the\nbroad state policies of the Public Service Commission's Energy Efficien-\ncy Portfolio Standard program and NYSERDA's energy efficiency programs.\nFinally, as these systems are manufactured in New York State, the\nexpanded usage will encourage economic investment in skilled New York\njobs.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: 2008: A.8990-B - Referred to Assembly Calendar -\nRules Report 215\n2007: A.8990 - Referred to Assembly Energy Committee\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This bill shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
